8278 1077 "selection" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548278 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548311 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548278 1079 #uuid "1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548270 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548273 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548289 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548298 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548312 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1077 "selection" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548273 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548279 1079 #uuid "1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548332 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548280 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548280 1072 #uuid "14caf42e-c7a2-5980-adda-a477fd9e1d63" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548280 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548281 1080 "144 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548281 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548281 1083 #uuid "19c9f542-d2e2-5968-bded-427e0d8db6a3" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548287 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548282 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548282 1072 #uuid "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548282 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548288 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548309 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548323 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548325 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548330 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1077 "selection" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548330 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548283 1079 #uuid "1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548284 1080 "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548284 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548284 1083 #