the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548301 1072 #uuid "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548301 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548319 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548302 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548302 1072 #uuid "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548302 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548303 1080 "81 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548303 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548303 1083 #uuid "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548304 1080 "0" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548304 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548304 1083 #uuid "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548293 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548305 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548305 1072 #uuid "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548305 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548326 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548306 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $\\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548306 1072 #uuid "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548306 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548307 1080 "54 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548307 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548307 1083 #uuid "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548308 1080 "- 612 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548308 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548308 1083 #uuid "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548309 1080 "36 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548309 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548309 1083 #uuid "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548331 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548310 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548310 1072 #uuid "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548310 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548311 1080 "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548311 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548311 1083 #uuid "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548312 1080 "96 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548312 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548312 1083 #uuid "90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548313 1080 "18 \\pi" 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548313 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535958 17592186045443 true] [17592186548313 1083 #uuid "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" 13